{"id":"4239996","slug":"travis-etienne-jr","name":"Travis Etienne Jr.","position":"RB","team":"NO","headshot":"https://a.espncdn.com/i/headshots/nfl/players/full/4239996.png","formats":{"ppr-12":{"positionRank":14,"ourRank":40,"vorRound":4,"adp":37,"gap":-3,"projected":182.94,"vor":34.78,"expectedGames":14.8,"floor":123.05,"median":182.87,"ceiling":242.69,"tier":3,"tierDrop":18.8}},"capsule":{"body":"The IR tag as of mid-October is the first thing to square before anything else, and the games number already knows about it: 15 expected, with a real range of 10 to 17. If he plays the full slate, the 14.9 points a game he averaged in 2025 makes him a steal around pick 36. If he sits a month, you drafted an RB whose 183 projected points shrink with every week he misses.\n\nThe production itself is easy to trust. He went from 8.68 points a game in 2024 to 14.94 in 2025, played all 17 weeks, and finished RB10 on 296 touches. Inside the scoring area he was RB6 in red-zone touches and RB6 in touches inside the ten, which is why a move from Jacksonville to New Orleans matters less than it would for most backs. He cleared the position's weekly median in 15 of his 17 games last year, and his best week, a 31.5-point outing in week 15, shows the ceiling is real. Kamara sits behind him on the depth chart, so the role is his to lose. The worry is efficiency, not role: 30.7% of his points came on touchdowns, worth minus 6.5 a game net of the factors we track, and his 20.5% red-zone touch share ranks RB28 because so much of his work sits outside the ten.\n\nHe is the last of his tier before an 18.8-point drop, our number puts him at 183, and that is about 11 points clear of the pool of backs drafted where he goes. We would take him at his ADP and not a pick sooner, with the IR news checked before you submit the card.","pulls":[],"writtenAt":"2026-10-13T22:12:55.885Z"},"weekRead":{"week":6,"body":"Travis Etienne Jr. does not play in week 6 of 2026: he sits on injured reserve on Tuesd